# Skill Authoring

Use this rule whenever creating, editing, reviewing, or packaging skills in this repo.

## Before Creating Or Editing

- First decide whether the work is a new skill, an improvement to an existing skill, or an orchestrator.
- Check `skills/skill-creator/references/existing-skills.md` before creating anything new.
- Prefer extending or composing existing skills over creating parallel skills with overlapping jobs.
- Capture intent before drafting: trigger conditions, inputs, output format, success criteria, edge cases, and dependencies.
- Do not package a skill into a plugin until it has been reviewed and has at least lightweight eval prompts.

## Skill Shape

Every skill should have:

- A directory under `skills/[skill-name]/`.
- A `SKILL.md` with YAML frontmatter containing `name` and `description`.
- A lowercase hyphenated name that matches the directory.
- A trigger-focused description starting with or clearly implying "Use when...".
- Clear sections for input, dependencies (skills, files, tools/MCPs), workflow/instructions, output, guardrails, and follow-on skills.
- Supporting `references/`, `scripts/`, or `assets/` only when they reduce `SKILL.md` size or provide reusable tooling.

## Description Quality

- Write descriptions for discovery, not as summaries of the whole workflow.
- Include concrete user phrases, contexts, and near-miss terms.
- Avoid over-broad triggers that hijack adjacent work.
- Add "use this instead when..." guidance in the body for overlapping skills.

## Instruction Quality

- Use imperative instructions.
- Explain why key steps matter so the agent can handle edge cases intelligently.
- Keep child skills focused on one job.
- Keep orchestrator skills focused on sequencing, handoffs, checkpoints, and validation.
- State assumptions and missing inputs; do not invent facts, owners, dates, research evidence, or tool access.
- Add confirmation gates before side effects: writes, deploys, sends, bookmark mutations, Figma edits, or project-memory updates.

## Evaluation

- Add 2-3 realistic eval prompts before marking a skill as refined.
- Include both should-trigger and near-miss prompts when updating descriptions.
- For skill systems, eval the handoffs as well as each child skill.
- Use the eval viewer/workspace flow from `skill-creator` when doing substantial rewrites.
